Q: Why does my ASP.NET Core seed method only add sample data when the admin user does not exist? A: <p>the problem is that you have tied your sample data to the existence of the admin user. Your code basically says: If there is no admin, create one and then (implicitly) seed the rest.</p>
<p>once the admin is created, that entire if-block is skipped in every future run. To fix this, you need to check for products and rewards independently using their own context.</p>
<pre><code>public static async Task InitializeAsync(IServiceProvider serviceProvider)
{
    var context = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService&lt;ApplicationDbContext&gt;();
    var userManager = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService&lt;UserManager&lt;IdentityUser&gt;&gt;();

    await context.Database.MigrateAsync();

    // Admin logic - keep this separate
    var adminUser = await userManager.FindByEmailAsync(&quot;admin@example.com&quot;);
    if (adminUser == null)
    {
        // create admin logic
    }

    // Products logic - must be outside the admin check
    if (!context.Products.Any())
    {
        context.Products.AddRange(new List&lt;Product&gt; 
        { 
            new Product { Name = &quot;Sample&quot;, Price = 10 } 
        });
    }

    // Rewards logic - also independent
    if (!context.Rewards.Any())
    {
        // seed rewards
    }

    await context.SaveChangesAsync();
}
</code></pre>
<p>The issue isn't that EF Core is failing, it's just that your conditional logic uses the admin user as a gatekeeper for the whole process. By checking each table specifically with .Any(), the method becomes idempotent and will fill in whatever is missing regardless of who exists in the Identity tables.</p>

Q: JavaScript Media Session API: pause works, play doesn&#39;t work A: <p>If <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XY_problem" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the goal</a> is to allow unpausing the playback via the browser media controls, <strong>the pause button can be programmed to alternatingly pause <em>and</em> unpause</strong>.</p>
<p>Only users whose habits we can change by informing them about this &quot;feature&quot;, or users whose habits haven't solidified yet, will do this. And it's <strong>incosistent with other web apps</strong>. But in some cases this might be <strong>better than <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79926020/javascript-media-session-api-pause-works-play-doesnt-work#comment141038465_79926136">other risks</a></strong>.</p>
<p>Users can be informed about this via <code>mediaSession.metadata</code>. Note that they might not see the page while using media controls.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>if ('mediaSession' in navigator) {
  let audioCtx, oscillator, gainNode;
  let metadataPaused = new MediaMetadata({title: "⚠️click pause again to resume⚠️"});
  let metadataPlaying = new MediaMetadata({title: ""});

  // still have a 'play' handler for browsers in which pausing works
  navigator.mediaSession.setActionHandler('play', () =&gt; {
    audioCtx.resume();
    navigator.mediaSession.playbackState = 'playing';
  });

  // pause button with two purposes depending on playbackState
  navigator.mediaSession.setActionHandler('pause', () =&gt; {
    if (navigator.mediaSession.playbackState == 'playing') {
      audioCtx.suspend();
      navigator.mediaSession.playbackState = 'paused';
      navigator.mediaSession.metadata = metadataPaused;
    }
    else {
      audioCtx.resume();
      navigator.mediaSession.playbackState = 'playing';
      navigator.mediaSession.metadata = metadataPlaying;
    }
  });

  // set some `duration` so that the seek bar serves
  // as an additional indicator of the paused state
  if ('setPositionState' in navigator.mediaSession) {
    navigator.mediaSession.setPositionState({
      duration: 20,
      position: 0,
    });
  }

  button.onclick = async () =&gt; {
    if (!audioCtx) {
      audioCtx = new AudioContext();
      oscillator = audioCtx.createOscillator();
      gainNode = audioCtx.createGain();
      oscillator.connect(gainNode);
      gainNode.connect(audioCtx.destination);
      oscillator.frequency.value = 440;
      gainNode.gain.value = 0.1;
      oscillator.start();
      await audioCtx.resume();
      navigator.mediaSession.playbackState = 'playing';
    }
  };
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;button id="button"&gt;Initialize&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Javascript DOM .querySelector() null issue A: <p>Nowadays you can use optional chaining:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>document.querySelector('.tab.active')?.classList.remove('active');
//                                   ^ only change here

console.log('class after:', document.querySelector('.tab').className);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.active {
  background: red;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="tab active"&gt;hello world&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>This will be a no-op rather than a crash if <code>querySelector()</code> doesn't find a match:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>document.querySelector('.tab.active')?.classList.remove('active');
console.log('class after:', document.querySelector('.tab').className);</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.active {
  background: red;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- note: no 'active' class, so querySelector will not match this! --&gt;
&lt;div class="tab"&gt;hello world&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note that since <code>.remove()</code> is guaranteed to be defined on the DOM node if it does exist, you don't need to add <code>?.</code> to anything after <code>?.classList</code>.</p>

Q: How to instantiate a connected PowerSyncDatabase by calling a suspend function inside a synchronized block? A: <p>I'm not sure why you don't use Hilt as established in your <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79924686/">previous question</a>, but if you insist you can also acquire an instance manually, of course.</p>
<p>In that case, just move the <code>connect()</code> call outside of the synchronized block:</p>
<pre><code>private var instance: PowerSyncDatabase? = null

suspend fun getDatabase(applicationContext: Context): PowerSyncDatabase =
    synchronized(this) {
        instance?.let { return@getDatabase it }

        val driverFactory = DatabaseDriverFactory(applicationContext)
        PowerSyncDatabase(
            factory = driverFactory,
            schema = AppSchema,
            dbFilename = &quot;powersync.db&quot;,
        ).also { instance = it }
    }.apply {
        val supabaseClient = createSupabaseClient(
            supabaseUrl = dotenv[&quot;SUPABASE_URL&quot;],
            supabaseKey = dotenv[&quot;SUPABASE_KEY&quot;],
        ) {
            install(Postgrest)
            install(Auth)
        }

        connect(
            SupabaseConnector(
                supabaseClient = supabaseClient,
                powerSyncEndpoint = dotenv[&quot;POWERSYNC_URL&quot;],
            )
        )
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Now the connection is started <em>after</em> the <code>synchronized</code> block finished, so you don't need to suspend <em>inside</em> the <code>synchronized</code> block anymore - which the error message was warning you about.</p>
<p>Please note that I also moved the check if the instance already exists <em>inside</em> the <code>synchronized</code> block. Otherwise two threads simultaneously calling this function would both detect that <code>instance</code> is null and then both enter the <code>synchronized</code> block. Although that would happen one after another due to the block being synchronized, they would still both run the same code, creating two separate PowerSyncDatabase instances. With the fixed code the <code>synchronized</code> block will immediately exit the entire <code>getDatabase()</code> function if the instance was already initialized (and subsequently also connected) by a prior call, preserving the required singleton semantic.</p>
<p>Well, if you were still using Hilt it would have been as easy as just applying the <code>@Singleton</code> annotation.</p>
